incident :the_priced_return do
  name "The Priced Return"
  tags :prediction, :autonomy, :trade, :identity

  prominence :marginal

  prose <<~PROSE
    In the 2088 season a travelling group of thirty-one, calling itself Long Grass that year, took gallery and barge work at the #{ref :conowingo_engagement_counter, "Conowingo counter"} and filed four #{ref :the_engagement_allotment, "allotment notes"}. All four named allottees at standing addresses in Market towns, and all four paid on the first and the sixteenth. The remainder of the price stood in #{ref :claim_escrow, "escrow"} against identity records the group did not intend to produce.

    The group returned on the ninth of July 2089, having chosen the crossing by dice. The day board carried gallery work brought forward from September at a rate a fifth above the corridor's, posted six days earlier.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :course, heading: "Two Accounts Of One Rate"
    #{ref :market, "Market"}'s account is on the file and is not disputed by anybody who has read it. Gallery work was pulled forward because a supply of unskilled crews was expected in the second week of July, the expectation was priced, and the price was posted publicly six days in advance as every rate in the yard is. No model named the group and no instruction concerned it.

    The group's account is that four payment calendars and four standing addresses put a season's movements inside a week without anyone needing to be named, and that arriving by dice at a gate where the work has been made attractive is the condition #{ref :the_pilgrims, "the culture"} exists in order not to be in.

    They took the work. The rate was real, the galleries needed clearing, and the crew came off in the third week with the highest day money the group had earned in four seasons.
  PROSE

  prose <<~PROSE, section: :aftermath, heading: "What The Season Settled"
    Over the following winter most groups on the corridor adopted three rules: an allottee is a person the travelling group does not visit, notes are surrendered at the end of a season and not at the end of an engagement, and no two consecutive seasons draw against the same name. A fourth is argued about and not settled — whether a group that has drawn at a counter should return to it at all, since a route chosen at random into a destination somebody has already priced is not a random route.

    Long Grass no longer exists under that name. Its remainder does: Market's accounts carry the unclaimed balance of the 2089 engagement in #{year :now} CE, against a crew of nine recorded at the sluice yard and no recognized worker unpaid.
  PROSE

  log "2026-08-09 — The consequence follows from the payment calendar rather than from any observation of the group, because the allotment note's real-world ancestor pays on fixed days to a fixed address. A fixed payee is what makes the instrument survive a rule against assignment, and it is the same property that makes a season forecastable."
end
